WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.245 --> 00:00:05.161
Okay, so that would be a big reaction.

00:00:17.236 --> 00:00:18.148
My name is Tony Vincent,

00:00:18.148 --> 00:00:20.423
I'm a stunt coordinator
in the film industry.

00:00:20.423 --> 00:00:22.089
It was an accident!

00:00:22.089 --> 00:00:23.339
Listen to me!

00:00:28.104 --> 00:00:29.053
Lets go.

00:00:38.505 --> 00:00:40.306
After about 100 movies or so,

00:00:40.306 --> 00:00:42.236
I started doing fight choreography.

00:00:42.236 --> 00:00:43.886
I have a deep martial arts background.

00:00:43.886 --> 00:00:46.725
Everybody's either known as
a fight guy, a wheel guy,

00:00:46.725 --> 00:00:48.072
or a gymnast, a fall guy.

00:00:48.072 --> 00:00:49.933
Fighting has always been my specialty.

00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:51.682
I'm competent at the other skills,

00:00:51.682 --> 00:00:54.143
but I've always been
known more as a fight guy,

00:00:54.143 --> 00:00:56.289
and sought out for my fight skills.

00:00:56.289 --> 00:00:58.090
When it comes to taking a
man with your bare hands,

00:00:58.090 --> 00:00:59.473
it's rather risky isn't it?

00:00:59.473 --> 00:01:01.717
James Cagney did a movie,
"Blood on the Sun."

00:01:01.717 --> 00:01:04.100
And Judo was featured there.

00:01:08.978 --> 00:01:10.890
Every era saw different things.

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:13.687
You know, the '70s was
obviously the Bruce Lee era,

00:01:13.687 --> 00:01:16.270
and more of the Kung Fu flicks.

00:01:20.773 --> 00:01:23.119
So then you know, I think "Bourne" kind of

00:01:23.119 --> 00:01:26.101
changed some things, and
that was heavily based on

00:01:26.101 --> 00:01:28.198
the Filipino art, Kali and Escrima,

00:01:28.198 --> 00:01:29.300
and the stick and knife fighting.

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:30.884
So that kind of, I think, started

00:01:30.884 --> 00:01:33.944
that different style of
fighting, was more realistic.

00:01:33.944 --> 00:01:36.308
And I think it's here to stay.

00:01:36.308 --> 00:01:41.308
"Raid: Redemption" series was
also done fantastically well.

00:01:41.841 --> 00:01:45.091
"John Wick" was brilliant,
it was very heavy Judo-based.

00:01:45.091 --> 00:01:47.091
They brought an art that was not really

00:01:47.091 --> 00:01:49.174
shown that much in films.

00:01:50.389 --> 00:01:53.472
Now go home and get your shine box.

00:01:58.673 --> 00:01:59.964
One thing that I learned from my dad,

00:01:59.964 --> 00:02:02.082
since he was an actor, is

00:02:02.082 --> 00:02:03.593
what motivates the character?

00:02:03.593 --> 00:02:04.797
Why is he fighting?

00:02:04.797 --> 00:02:06.240
What is his ultimate goal?

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:09.861
Otherwise, it's just punches
and kicks and throws and locks.

00:02:09.861 --> 00:02:10.811
So that's the first step.

00:02:10.811 --> 00:02:14.644
The second step is,
what's the shooting style?

00:02:14.864 --> 00:02:17.531
Do they like to hold shots wide?

00:02:18.044 --> 00:02:21.790
Do they like to do a lot of
insert shots, flash cuts?

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:23.158
Because that'll help me know

00:02:23.158 --> 00:02:26.328
whether I really need
to have a flow of moves,

00:02:26.328 --> 00:02:29.046
or if it's gonna be cut
together in snippets.

00:02:29.046 --> 00:02:30.832
Then we shoot.

00:02:30.832 --> 00:02:32.238
We're gonna do a little
demonstration here,

00:02:32.238 --> 00:02:33.616
and go over some of the techniques

00:02:33.616 --> 00:02:35.096
to make fight scenes work.

00:02:35.096 --> 00:02:37.760
We have here Ian and we have Aj.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:39.368
The first thing we're gonna do

00:02:39.368 --> 00:02:41.495
is put Ian to sleep, okay?

00:02:41.495 --> 00:02:42.998
No, just kidding, okay.
(laughs)

00:02:42.998 --> 00:02:46.031
What we really have to talk
about is selling to camera.

00:02:46.031 --> 00:02:49.864
The camera, when stacked,
we're stacking right now,

00:02:49.864 --> 00:02:51.781
it compresses the space in between.

00:02:51.781 --> 00:02:54.492
So really, whether I'm here

00:02:54.492 --> 00:02:56.620
or whether I'm here

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:59.091
or if I actually hit him,
it all looks the same.

00:02:59.091 --> 00:03:00.231
Just to explain what I'm saying,

00:03:00.231 --> 00:03:02.597
if I went like this now, guess what?

00:03:02.597 --> 00:03:05.612
Well, that doesn't work,
we see a mile in between.

00:03:05.612 --> 00:03:07.820
There's a couple of things
that I call "choreo-killers."

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:08.888
What we call bridging.

00:03:08.888 --> 00:03:10.484
You guys are my friends.

00:03:10.484 --> 00:03:12.752
I don't want to hit you in the face.

00:03:12.752 --> 00:03:16.371
What a lot of people do is they go -

00:03:16.371 --> 00:03:17.503
I call it bridging. Why?

00:03:17.503 --> 00:03:20.237
We make it look like
it's the Verrazano, okay?

00:03:20.237 --> 00:03:22.391
Because in our mind, subconsciously,

00:03:22.391 --> 00:03:23.636
I don't want to hit you.

00:03:23.636 --> 00:03:25.073
What makes a fight scene work

00:03:25.073 --> 00:03:28.062
is not the person throwing the punch,

00:03:28.062 --> 00:03:29.788
it's the person receiving the punch.

00:03:29.788 --> 00:03:33.379
I always say, you want to
pretend that there is a pole

00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:34.736
going through the top of your head

00:03:34.736 --> 00:03:36.021
and running through your body.

00:03:36.021 --> 00:03:38.744
So the first thing that
happens is your head goes.

00:03:38.744 --> 00:03:40.844
Once your head goes, your shoulder goes,

00:03:40.844 --> 00:03:42.357
then your hip, then your knee.

00:03:42.357 --> 00:03:45.326
Now I really want you to
really throw it really hard.

00:03:45.326 --> 00:03:48.210
Obviously, don't hit me, 'cause
I have meetings after this.

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:49.043
(laughs)

00:03:49.043 --> 00:03:49.943
So go ahead.

00:03:49.943 --> 00:03:50.776
Beautiful, okay good.
- Alright.

00:03:50.776 --> 00:03:52.276
Now do it again.

00:03:55.065 --> 00:03:57.166
Okay, so that would be a big reaction.

00:03:57.166 --> 00:04:00.825
You are going to punch
him in the face, okay?

00:04:00.825 --> 00:04:03.280
And then punch him in the stomach.

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:04.540
Then what I want you to do is

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:07.713
throw a right punch at his ear.

00:04:07.713 --> 00:04:09.271
Someone's getting workers' comp today.

00:04:09.271 --> 00:04:10.602
(laughter)
- Right, right, right.

00:04:10.602 --> 00:04:13.428
Now again, this is a famous actor.

00:04:13.428 --> 00:04:14.684
- You don't wanna hit him,

00:04:14.684 --> 00:04:15.517
but you don't want to look

00:04:15.517 --> 00:04:16.526
like you're throwing a little punch.

00:04:16.526 --> 00:04:18.552
We're gonna do a half-speed rehearsal.

00:04:18.552 --> 00:04:19.385
- Okay.
- Okay?

00:04:19.385 --> 00:04:21.449
So half speed to feel it out
and know what you're gonna do,

00:04:21.449 --> 00:04:22.912
and then we'll go full-speed, okay?

00:04:22.912 --> 00:04:24.430
Alright, so I walk up,
like, "You got a problem?"

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:26.363
What's your problem?
- Hold on, hold on a second.

00:04:26.363 --> 00:04:27.442
Did I say "action?"

00:04:27.442 --> 00:04:28.796
Oh, you did not say "action."

00:04:28.796 --> 00:04:29.949
So you're just running
the set on your own?

00:04:29.949 --> 00:04:31.109
It's my first time on set!

00:04:31.109 --> 00:04:33.428
- Okay, okay!
(laughter)

00:04:33.428 --> 00:04:34.495
Ready, and action.

00:04:34.495 --> 00:04:35.892
Alright, you got a problem?

00:04:35.892 --> 00:04:36.921
You got a problem, man, huh?

00:04:36.921 --> 00:04:38.847
Nice and slow, nice and slow.

00:04:38.847 --> 00:04:40.388
Yeah, wham, good!

00:04:40.388 --> 00:04:41.221
Good!

00:04:42.438 --> 00:04:43.799
Good, good.

00:04:43.799 --> 00:04:44.756
It's the full-speed.

00:04:44.756 --> 00:04:46.389
Alright.

00:04:46.389 --> 00:04:47.912
Cameras are rolling.

00:04:47.912 --> 00:04:49.869
- Whoo!
- Okay, here we go.

00:04:49.869 --> 00:04:52.408
Ready, and action.

00:04:52.408 --> 00:04:53.666
'Sup, man, you got a problem?

00:04:53.666 --> 00:04:57.749
You got a problem, man,
what's your problem, man?

00:05:01.149 --> 00:05:02.538
Nice, very nice!

00:05:02.538 --> 00:05:04.182
Very nice, good job, guys.

00:05:04.182 --> 00:05:05.015
Good good good.

00:05:05.015 --> 00:05:06.520
What's your availability next week?

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:07.853
I have a project for you.
- I'm free!

00:05:07.853 --> 00:05:08.861
You're free?

00:05:08.861 --> 00:05:09.694
Yeah, okay.

00:05:09.694 --> 00:05:11.284
(laughs)

00:05:11.284 --> 00:05:12.117
Thank you guys.

00:05:12.117 --> 00:05:13.117
Thank you.

